# Revelle Société — environment
#
# Copy to .env.local for development. NEVER commit the filled-in version;
# .gitignore already excludes .env* except this file.
#
# There are no local fallbacks by design: a missing value fails loudly at the
# point of use rather than quietly working on one machine only. The same rule
# is why migrations run inside Render (see render.yaml) and not from a laptop.

# ── Database ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# REQUIRED. Postgres connection string.
# SSL is inferred from the host: anything that is not localhost/127.0.0.1/[::1]
# gets TLS, because hosted providers reject plaintext connections. Append
# ?sslmode=disable to force it off (the only escape hatch, honoured by both
# src/lib/db.ts and scripts/migrate.mjs).
#
# On Render this is injected from the database's connectionString — do not set
# it by hand there.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@host:5432/revelle

# Optional. Pool size per process, default 10. Set it below the database plan's
# connection limit if more than one instance runs.
# DATABASE_POOL_MAX=10

# ── Email (Resend) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# REQUIRED for the quiz confirmation. Without them the quiz still saves her
# answers — the submission is never failed by a mail problem — and the send is
# skipped with a warning in the log.
#
# MAIL_FROM must be on a domain verified in the Resend account, or every send
# is rejected with a 4xx.
RESEND_API_KEY=re_xxxxxxxxxxxx
MAIL_FROM="Revelle Société <hello@revellesociete.com>"

# Optional. Where a customer's reply goes. The confirmation email tells her to
# reply, so this should be an address a human actually reads.
# MAIL_REPLY_TO=studio@revellesociete.com

# ── The soundtrack (Spotify) ─────────────────────────────────────────
# OPTIONAL, and its absence is a ROUTE rather than a fault: with none of these
# set, every soundtrack is delivered as a printed setlist and a line in The
# Prep, which is a real deliverable and the same thing a customer with no
# subscription receives. Nothing fails, nothing half-builds. Same discipline as
# the Resend keys above.
#
# WHAT THE FOUNDER MUST OBTAIN, IN THIS ORDER
#
#   1. A Spotify account that IS the house — not a personal one. Every playlist
#      ever sent to a customer lives in it, publicly and permanently. Its user
#      id is SPOTIFY_HOUSE_USER_ID; the authorisation script prints it.
#      (Premium is not needed to CREATE playlists. It is what SHE needs to hear
#      one in order on a phone, which is why the application asks.)
#   2. Signed in as that account: an app at
#      https://developer.spotify.com/dashboard → Client ID and Client Secret.
#   3. In that app's settings, a Redirect URI of exactly
#      http://127.0.0.1:8888/callback — the literal loopback IP. Spotify no
#      longer accepts "localhost", and says only "INVALID_CLIENT".
#   4. `npm run spotify:authorize`, once, on a machine with a browser. It walks
#      the consent screen and prints the refresh token and the date.
#
SPOTIFY_CLIENT_ID=
SPOTIFY_CLIENT_SECRET=
# The société's own account. A PARAMETER everywhere in src/lib/music/, never a
# constant: an account holds about 11,000 playlists, and a second one is meant
# to be a config change rather than a rewrite.
SPOTIFY_HOUSE_USER_ID=
#
# ⚠  THE SIX-MONTH CLOCK — the one thing about this integration that will bite.
#
# A refresh token lives about SIX MONTHS and REFRESHING AN ACCESS TOKEN DOES
# NOT EXTEND IT. Six months of the app quietly renewing hourly access tokens
# ends on exactly the day this token was always going to die — and when it
# does, playlist creation fails for a customer whose evening is on Saturday.
#
# Re-run `npm run spotify:authorize` before then, and put
# `npm run spotify:verify` on a schedule: it exits non-zero when the token is
# dead or within a month of it. Failed handovers are also rows —
# `select * from tracklist_rendering where status = 'failed'` (db/005).
SPOTIFY_REFRESH_TOKEN=
# The date the token above was issued (YYYY-MM-DD), printed by the script.
# Optional, and worth setting: it is the only thing that can warn BEFORE the
# expiry rather than after it.
SPOTIFY_REFRESH_TOKEN_OBTAINED=

# Optional. An ISO country code. When set, Spotify returns the pressing that is
# actually playable there. A WRONG value hides catalogue that exists, so leave
# it unset unless the house knows which market it is selecting for.
# SPOTIFY_MARKET=US

# Optional. Only read by scripts/spotify-authorize.mjs, and only useful if port
# 8888 is taken. Must match the dashboard exactly, and must be a 127.0.0.1 URL.
# SPOTIFY_REDIRECT_URI=http://127.0.0.1:8888/callback

# ── URLs ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The real public origin, no trailing slash. Used for absolute links in
# outbound mail and, later, for a customer's Revelle URL. A wrong value
# produces links that 404 for the recipient.
APP_URL=http://localhost:3000

# ── The desk (internal) ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# WHO MAY SIGN IN. Comma-separated addresses; case and surrounding spaces do
# not matter. Read by src/lib/staff.ts on every request, so removing an address
# ends that person's session at her next click rather than in a fortnight.
#
# IT FAILS CLOSED. Unset or empty means NOBODY is staff and /desk is
# unreachable — there is no "any signed-in user" fallback and no development
# default, the same discipline as DATABASE_URL above.
#
# It is an environment variable and not a table on purpose: these are real
# personal addresses that do not belong in git, and the list has to be
# changeable on Render without a deploy. The `staff` table (db/011) is the
# REGISTER — it exists so a change has an author — not the gate.
#
# Sign-in is passwordless: a link, good once, for fifteen minutes. It goes out
# through Resend, so RESEND_API_KEY and MAIL_FROM above are what make the desk
# reachable. With no mail key, a development server prints the link to its own
# log instead; in production a missing key is a fault and says so.
#
# THERE IS ONE DOOR, at /login, and this variable is what decides which side of
# it somebody comes out on: an address named here is sent to /desk, and any
# other address that belongs to an accepted member is sent to her portal. The
# person typing is never told which — or whether she is on file at all. See
# db/015 and src/lib/login.ts.
STAFF_EMAILS=

# ── Background work ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The jobs table is db/008 and the runner is src/lib/jobs/. It starts itself
# from src/instrumentation.ts inside the web process — there is no worker
# service and nothing here has to be run from anyone's machine. Every value
# below is OPTIONAL and the defaults are the intended production settings.

# Set to `off` to stop this instance claiming work. The one reason to: running
# a second copy of the app against the same database for a migration or a
# debugging session, where you do not want two runners both taking jobs.
# JOBS_RUNNER=off

# How many jobs one pass takes, which is also how many run at once. Four is the
# size of a Revelle's fan-out, so one generation's pieces run in parallel
# without one generation monopolising the process.
# JOBS_BATCH_SIZE=4

# How often to look for work, in milliseconds. Short enough that a customer
# watching a page does not notice the poll; an idle queue costs one indexed
# lookup per interval.
# JOBS_INTERVAL_MS=2000

# Registers the fixture job types (fixture.echo, fixture.fail, fixture.flaky,
# fixture.fanout) that exercise the queue with no language model involved. For
# development and for the database-backed tests. NOT for production.
# JOBS_FIXTURES=on

# Only read by src/lib/jobs/queue.db.test.ts, and deliberately NOT DATABASE_URL:
# every test in that file truncates the job table, so it must be impossible to
# point it at anything that matters by forgetting to set something.
#   JOBS_TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgres://localhost/revelle_jobs_test npm test
# Unset, those tests skip and `npm test` needs no database at all.
# JOBS_TEST_DATABASE_URL=

# ── The desk digest ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A read-only bearer token for GET /api/desk/digest, which returns the open
# to-dos and the curators' thread. It exists because the database has an empty
# ipAllowList and is reachable only from inside Render — so this is the only
# way anything outside the service can see what the desk has asked for.
#
# FAILS CLOSED: unset or empty means the route answers 404, not that it is
# open. Same rule as STAFF_EMAILS.
#
# It grants exactly one read. It is NOT a way to be a curator, it cannot
# mutate anything, and it returns no member data and no application content.
# Generate with: node -e 'console.log(require("crypto").randomBytes(32).toString("base64url"))'
DESK_DIGEST_TOKEN=
